      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is \"event timestamp\" referring to the __hw_clock_event_get() value?\n\nI want to make sure that I understand how the timer code is supposed to work. It looks like process_timers() handles scheduling future interrupts by calling __hw_event_clock_set with the next pending deadline.\n\n__hw_event_clock_set() then sets the \"capture and control\" register (STM32_TIM32_CCR1) so that it will generate an interrupt when this future deadline is hit: https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/chip/stm32/hwtimer32.c;l\u003d23-28;drc\u003d604f168378ac5e896a23403c4e78d18da62baaba.\n\nIt looks like the TIM_CLOCK32 timer is configured to count up and trigger interrupt on overflow: https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/chip/stm32/hwtimer32.c;l\u003d221-225;drc\u003d604f168378ac5e896a23403c4e78d18da62baaba.\n\nSo the way I\u0027m picturing this is that TIM_CLOCK32 is always running (periodically overflowing \u003d\u003e generating interrupt which increments clksrc_high). The value in STM32_TIM32_CCR1 does not change by itself (only when process_timer calls __hw_clock_event_set after the deadline triggers the match interrupt). \n\nI think this is exactly what you\u0027re describing here, so I\u0027m adding some comments on the lines that I think could be a little clearer.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 481,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 489,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "4b8343179db0e8657229c7474013b9b70e344d8f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
